<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wube Haile Maryam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. <a href="/years/1831">1831</a>–<a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wube Haile Maryam was the lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semien">Semien</a> who also ruled Tigray from 1831 to 1855, making him one of the strongest regional rulers of the Ethiopian Era of Princes. He sought European contacts and firearms to strengthen his position. Defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosice">Kassa</a> Hailu at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-deresge">Battle of Deresge</a> in 1855, he was imprisoned by Kassa, now Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a>, and spent the rest of his life in captivity.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wube-haile-maryam.jpg" alt="Wube Haile Maryam" width="320" height="512" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alphonse-Marie-Adolphe de Neuville · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ras_Wub%C3%A9_of_Tigre.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Wube was born in 1799 into an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amhara">Amhara</a> family of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/semien">Semien</a> mountains, the son of Haile Maryam Gebre, governor of Semien, and of Mintayé of Jan Amora, a side marriage of his father. His half-brother Betul was the father of Taytu Betul, later empress and wife of Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>. Haile Maryam chose another son, Merso, to succeed him, but because Merso's mother was of Yejju origin, the officers of Semien chose Wube, whose mother was a native of the province.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1827 Wube was defeated by Ras Yimam and Dejazmach Maru of Dembiya and lost Wogera, but later that year he helped Yimam defeat Maru and Goshu of Gojjam at Kosso Ber. He married Dinqinesh, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabagadis-woldu">Sabagadis Woldu</a>, ruler of Tigray, yet in 1830 Sabagadis drove him from his fortresses. In February 1831 Wube and Ras Marye invaded Tigray; Marye fell, and Sabagadis was defeated at Debre Abbay and executed. Wube then subdued Tigray and in 1832 the Mereb Melash, where oral tradition remembers him as a ruthless warlord, and controlled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-sea">Red Sea</a>, though not <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>.</p><p>In 1838 he expelled Protestant missionaries and welcomed the Lazarist mission of Justin de Jacobis. Theophile Lefebvre led his delegation to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-philippe-i">Louis Philippe I</a> and returned in 1841 with guns and artisans, and a mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> brought back a new metropolitan, Abuna Salama III. In 1841 Wube defeated Ras Ali II and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gondar">Gondar</a>, but in February 1842 at Debre Tabor he was captured while drunk by Ali's uncle Birru Aligaz, which let Ali keep his title.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 9 February 1855 Kassa Hailu defeated and captured Wube. Within days Kassa was crowned Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tewodros-ii">Tewodros II</a> by Abuna Salama III in the Dirasge Mariam Church, which Wube had completed for his own coronation. Some sources date the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zemene-mesafint">Zemene Mesafint</a>, the Era of the Princes, to Wube's defeat, since he was the last remaining autonomous regional ruler. Wube died in chains in 1867.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Wube Haile Maryam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1855 Kassa Hailu defeated <b>Wube Haile Maryam</b>, lord of Tigray and Semien, at the Battle of Deresge and was crowned Tewodros II, ending the Era of Princes.</blockquote><a href="/state/tigray">Tigray</a></li></ul></section>
